export const FR_C1_S06_ENGLISH: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "fr-c1-le-car-rapide": [
    "I had been warned: in Dakar you don't take the car rapide, you conquer it. These old Renault minibuses, painted yellow and blue, covered with proverbs, protective eyes and portraits of marabouts, have been criss-crossing the city since the seventies. The authorities are gradually replacing them with new air-conditioned buses, safer, more punctual and infinitely less poetic. I wanted to ride in one before they disappeared altogether.",
    "The first one I hailed, at the Médina roundabout, had the word \"Alhamdoulilah\" on its bonnet and, on the back, a sentence in Wolof that nobody would give me an exact translation of, which speaks volumes. A young man was clinging to the back door, one foot on the step, the other in thin air: the apprenti. He is the one who shouts out the destinations, collects the coins, and signals the next stop to the driver by tapping a coin against the bodywork. Two knocks, you stop; one knock, you go.",
    "His name was Moussa, he was nineteen and had the agility of a trapeze artist. He hoisted me inside before the vehicle had even come to a stop. I landed on a wooden bench, wedged between a fish seller, a student absorbed in a constitutional-law textbook and a sheep, or more precisely two thirds of a sheep, the rest sticking out of a rice sack. Nobody seemed to find the situation unusual, and I did my best to look as unfazed as they did.",
    "The car rapide hardly deserves its name. It stops everywhere, sets off without warning, sits for twenty minutes in the traffic jams on the Avenue Blaise-Diagne, then suddenly charges off like a bull just let loose. Passengers get on, get off, squeeze up, pass their children to one another over people's heads. People discuss the price of onions, the last match of the Lions, a wedding where there were supposedly too many griots and not enough rice. The journey becomes a conversation in twenty voices.",
    "After an hour, I began to worry. The buildings of the Plateau, where I had an appointment, should have appeared long ago. Instead, wasteland went by, welding workshops, then, in the distance, the sea on one side and cement works on the other. I asked the student where we were. He looked up from his textbook, gazed at me with infinite compassion and announced that we were arriving in Rufisque, twenty-five kilometres from my destination.",
    "I had taken the wrong bus, or rather the right bus in the wrong direction. The whole bench got involved. The fish seller declared that it was Moussa's fault, he should have asked me where I was going; Moussa protested that I had looked as if I knew what I was doing, which was flattering but untrue. The student offered to go with me, so did the sheep's owner, with the sheep. In the end it was decided, by majority, that Moussa would look after me.",
    "At Rufisque, the end of the line, he jumped off the bus, guided me through the bus station to another vehicle leaving for Dakar, negotiated with his colleague, then settled me in the best seat, just behind the driver. When I tried to pay him for my first journey, he flatly refused. \"You've paid with your day,\" he said, laughing. \"That's enough.\" Then he knocked twice on the bodywork, and the bus lurched off.",
    "In Senegal people talk a lot about \"teranga\", the hospitality the country has made its pride. I had read the word in every guidebook. I did not yet know that it could fit into such a simple gesture: a nineteen-year-old boy losing half an hour's work to put a stranger back in the right direction. The new buses are no doubt more comfortable. I doubt they have any Moussas.",
  ],
  "fr-c1-le-traversier-de-tadoussac": [
    "The ferry linking Baie-Sainte-Catherine and Tadoussac takes only ten minutes to cross the mouth of the Saguenay. It is free, leaves every twenty minutes, and guidebooks present it as a mere formality on Route 138. That is a mistake. Those ten minutes are among the most beautiful you can spend in Quebec, provided you get out of your car and climb up to the deck, which almost nobody does.",
    "That August morning there were only two of us, leaning on the rail in a wind that smelled of salt and resin. My neighbour wore a red windbreaker, binoculars round her neck, and that quiet confidence of people who are at home. She must have noticed from my accent that I came from France, because she came up to me without any preamble. \"You're pretty lucky, you. The weather's nice. Usually in August we freeze solid up on deck.\"",
    "Her name was Ginette, she was seventy-eight, and she had been taking this ferry twice a day for more than forty years. I asked if she worked on the other side. She burst out laughing. \"I retired in 2009, my dear. I cross for nothing. Well, not for nothing.\" And she raised her binoculars towards the dark water of the fjord, where the current of the Saguenay, laden with earth and leaves, runs into the cold green waters of the St. Lawrence.",
    "During the ten minutes of the crossing, Ginette gave me a crash course in Quebec French that I am not about to forget. I learned that you don't \"faire les courses\", you \"magasiner\", that a car is a \"char\", that the corner shop is called a \"dépanneur\", and that a boring thing is \"plate\". I also learned that a French person who says \"super sympa\" every three sentences can be spotted a hundred metres away, which made me blush, as I had just said it twice.",
    "Then she suddenly fell silent and grabbed my arm. \"There. To the right of the buoy.\" At first I thought it was a wave, a crest of foam a little whiter than the others. Then the back surfaced again, smooth, milky, almost unreal, followed by a second one, smaller and greyish. \"A mother and her little one,\" Ginette murmured. \"The young one is grey until five or six. After that it turns white, like us.\"",
    "The St. Lawrence belugas are the last of their species to live so far south. There were more than ten thousand of them at the start of the twentieth century; today fewer than two thousand are counted, threatened by pollution, the noise of ships and the warming of the water. Ginette knows these figures by heart. For thirty years she was a technician at the research centre in Tadoussac. Since retiring, she has gone on noting, in a spiral notebook, every beluga she spots from the deck of the ferry.",
    "\"It's no use, I know that,\" she confided. \"The researchers have drones, hydrophones, things far more accurate than my old eyes.\" Yet she took out her notebook and wrote, in tiny handwriting: 9.40 a.m., two individuals, mother and calf, east of the buoy. \"But if I stop counting, I'm afraid I'll stop seeing them. And if there's nobody to see them, I'm afraid they'll go away for good.\"",
    "The ferry docked at Tadoussac with a rumble of chains. Ginette put away her notebook, wished me a good trip and advised me, if I found a boring moment during my stay, to come back and take the ferry. She was going to head back the other way on the next boat. I drove to Quebec City that day, but I never stopped thinking about her, her notebook, and that strange and beautiful idea that looking at something is already, in a way, protecting it.",
  ],
  "fr-c1-lettre-des-marquises": [
    "Atuona, Hiva Oa, November 2. My dear Mum, your letter of September 20 arrived yesterday with the Aranui, along with three tonnes of cement, a fridge for the Chinese grocer's, forty sacks of flour and a horse. The whole village was on the quay. Here the arrival of the cargo ship, every three weeks, serves as newspaper, funfair and postal service all at once. I read it sitting on a low wall while they unloaded the horse, which clearly had no wish to set foot on land.",
    "You ask me, for the fourth time since spring, when I am coming home. I'll get to that, I promise, but first let me tell you about my days, since you complain that you never know what I do at the end of the world. I am one of the two nurses at the Atuona clinic. The doctor comes once a month by plane from Nuku Hiva, when the weather allows. The rest of the time we stitch up, dress wounds, vaccinate, and decide on our own, sometimes at three in the morning, whether to call for a medical evacuation to Papeete, fourteen hundred kilometres away.",
    "I know that figure will worry you. It worried me too, the first few months. Then I understood that here, remoteness is not an absence, it is a way of life. People don't count on the State, or on the boat, or on the plane; they count on each other. When a woman goes into labour early, it is the fisherman next door who drives her to the clinic, the neighbour who looks after the older children, and the pastor who brings raw fish in coconut milk for the young mother.",
    "I have a patient I think you would like. His name is Teiki, he is eighty-one and he is a sculptor. He comes every week for his diabetes, which he treats with heroic nonchalance. He carves tikis in miro wood and volcanic stone, those little statues with huge eyes that are sold to the Aranui tourists. But he explained to me that a real tiki is not sold: it is given, and it keeps a little of the \"mana\" of the person who made it. I didn't understand it all, but I nodded with conviction.",
    "On Sundays I sometimes walk up to the little cemetery overlooking the bay. That is where Gauguin and Brel lie, a few metres from each other. There are always fresh flowers on their graves, garlands of tiaré, notes in every language. I sit in the shade of a frangipani tree and look at the ocean. I understand why these two men wanted to end their days here. It isn't that you forget the world here; it's that you stop needing it to look at you.",
    "So I come to your question. I won't be coming home for Christmas, Mum. I have signed on for another year. I know you will be disappointed, maybe even angry, and I understand. Don't think I am running away from something, or that I love you and Dad any less. It is simply that, for the first time in my life, I feel I am exactly where I belong, useful to people who repay me a hundredfold.",
    "On Tuesday, as he left his appointment, Teiki handed me a small parcel wrapped in a banana leaf. Inside was a tiki of grey stone, no bigger than my thumb. \"For your mother,\" he said. \"So she knows someone is watching over you.\" I am sending it to you with this letter. Put it on the mantelpiece, next to Granny's photo. I think they will get on well.",
    "And then, since I am not coming to you, I thought you could come to me. In the envelope you will also find a booking on board the Aranui, leaving Papeete on March 12. Thirteen days at sea, six islands, and your daughter on the quay when you arrive. Don't tell me it's too far. You taught me yourself that distance is just a question of boats. Lots of love. Your Lucie.",
  ],
  "fr-c1-une-nuit-a-ouessant": [
    "\"Whoever sees Ouessant sees his own blood,\" goes the proverb. Sailors have been repeating it for centuries, and since yesterday I understand why. The westernmost island of mainland France, twenty kilometres off the Finistère, is surrounded by some of the most violent currents in Europe, by reefs just beneath the surface and by a fog that comes down in a few minutes. I had come for two days of hiking. The storm had other plans.",
    "The return boat was cancelled at noon. The wind was blowing at more than a hundred kilometres an hour, the waves were slapping the jetty at Le Stiff, and the island's sheep, small and black, had all lain down in the shelter of the dry-stone walls, backs to the wind, with admirable fatalism. All that was left was to find a bed. Maïwenn, who runs a guesthouse in the village of Lampaul, took me in without asking questions, the way shipwrecked people have always been taken in here.",
    "In the evening the electricity cut out. Maïwenn lit candles, revived the stove, and we ate by the flickering light of the flames: a lamb stew cooked under clods of turf, an island speciality, and a bottle of cider. Her father, Yann, joined us. Eighty-four, a fisherman's jumper patched at the elbows, enormous hands. He was a lighthouse keeper for twenty-six years, eleven of them at La Jument, the lighthouse planted on a rock out at sea, battered by waves that sometimes rise as high as its lantern.",
    "I asked him what it was like to live shut up for weeks in a stone tower in the middle of the ocean. He took a long time to answer. \"We didn't live, we kept watch. It's not the same.\" He told me about the impossible changeovers, when the sea stopped boats from getting close and you stayed an extra month with the same tinned food and the same colleague. About the stormy nights when the whole tower shook, when the dishes fell off the shelves, and when you still had to climb the stairs to wind the mechanism every four hours.",
    "Then he spoke, more quietly, about the \"proëllas\". In the old days, when a sailor from Ouessant vanished at sea and his body was never found, a vigil was held at his home over a little wax cross, which took the place of the missing dead man. It was then placed in a mausoleum, in the church. \"There are hundreds of them,\" he said. \"Fathers, sons, brothers. My grandfather is one of them. It was for them we kept the light. So there would be no more wax crosses.\"",
    "La Jument lighthouse was automated in 1991. Yann was one of the last to leave it. Since then a machine has switched on the lantern, monitored it and reported its breakdowns to a computer in Brest. \"It does its job very well,\" he admitted without bitterness. \"Better than we did, no doubt. It never falls asleep.\" He paused. \"But it doesn't know who it's shining for.\"",
    "Around midnight, as the storm grew fiercer, I found him standing at the kitchen window, a candle in his hand. In the distance, through the rain, the beam of the Créac'h lighthouse was sweeping the night: two white flashes, then darkness, then two flashes again. Yann was counting under his breath. \"Two flashes every ten seconds. It's working.\" Maïwenn explained to me later that he had done this in every storm for thirty years. He only went to bed once he was sure all the island's lighthouses were turning.",
    "The next day the sea had calmed as if nothing had happened. The boat left at eleven, under a washed sky of an almost insolent blue. On deck I watched the island move away, its stone walls, its black sheep, its lighthouses standing like sentries. A great deal is written about automation replacing people. Less is written about the people who carry on, without anyone asking them to, doing the machines' work, just in case.",
  ],
  "fr-c1-le-volcan-poli": [
    "When the Piton de la Fournaise erupted, one Tuesday evening, the whole island of Réunion seemed to hold its breath, then get into its car. The volcano, one of the most active in the world, wakes up almost every year, and each time there is the same fervour: Réunion islanders drive up as families, with cool boxes and thermos flasks, to watch the lava spurt out in the night. I had spent three weeks hoping for this moment. I too rushed onto the Route des Plaines.",
    "I was not the only one to have had the idea. Twenty kilometres from the summit, the line of cars had stopped moving. At midnight the prefecture closed access to the caldera, for fear of gases and flying debris. I turned back, dejected, barely glimpsing, above the ridges, an orange glow that could have been a distant city. My guide, Johnny, a big strapping fellow from Saint-Pierre who punctuates all his sentences with a soothing \"lé bon\", promised to console me the next day.",
    "He took me to the east coast, to Sainte-Rose, where the volcano, when it overflows, ends its course in the ocean. The road crosses lava flows from different periods, black, frozen into waves, ropes, strange blisters. Some are as bare as on the first day; others have already been colonised by ferns and young casuarina trees. \"Here,\" Johnny explained, \"you always know how old the landscape is. You just have to look at what's growing on it.\"",
    "By the side of the road stands a little white church, Notre-Dame-des-Laves. In 1977 a lava flow hurtled down the slope, swallowed houses, cut the road, then stopped dead in front of the church door, went round it and continued on its way to the sea. You can still see the tongue of black rock frozen a few centimetres from the steps. The photos from the time went round the world. The newspapers spoke of a miracle.",
    "On a bench in front of the church, an old lady was shelling beans into a plastic basin. Johnny greeted her in Creole; her name was Mme Hoarau, she was ninety-one and had lived in the same little house all her life. She had seen the 1977 lava flow with her own eyes. I asked her, with the clumsiness of those who come from far away, whether she believed in the miracle. She laughed for a long time, without stopping her shelling.",
    "\"Miracle, miracle... The zoreils love that word.\" The zoreils, Johnny whispered to me, are people from mainland France, in other words me. \"Me, I think the volcano is polite. It came to the house of the Good Lord, it saw the door was shut, it went round. Like when you go to someone's house and nobody's in. You don't break the door down. You go through the garden.\" She looked at me with a mischievous eye. \"It broke my house, though. With me, it wasn't polite.\"",
    "She had lost her house in 1977, then rebuilt it in the same spot, a hundred metres further on. I asked her why she hadn't gone to live somewhere else, far from the volcano. She shrugged and dropped a handful of beans into the basin. \"Go where? Here the land is new. It's fifty years old, like my son. The volcano takes, and then it gives. You can't love the island and hate the volcano. It's the same thing.\"",
    "I never saw the eruption up close. It stopped three days later, before the road reopened. But what I keep of Réunion is that landscape where you read the age of the ground from the plants growing on it, that church which a river of fire avoided out of politeness, and that woman shelling her beans a hundred metres from the spot where she had lost everything, without resentment, the way you live next door to a difficult neighbour you have ended up loving.",
  ],
};

